ems like for size=4 (no refills), consumed always = ceil(total/8) which is always <= 4.\n\nBut the FIX shows that the old formula `data_index + (bit_index >> 3) + (bit_index > 0 ? 1 : 0)` was wrong. Let me see when it differs from `ceil(consumed_bits/8)`:\n\nIn the old code: consumed_bits is not tracked. The formula uses data_index and bit_index.\nIn the NEW code: consumed_bits is tracked and capped at size*8.\n\nThe issue must be that in the old code, data_index can grow BEYOND what's needed because:\n1. The init loads bytes 0..min(size-1, 3) but data_index starts at 0\n2. consume's while loop reads data[data_index + 4]\n\nThis indexing scheme means:\n- Byte 0 is loaded by init\n- Byte 4 is loaded by first while iteration (data_index=0)\n- data_index=0 means \"first byte past init\" is byte 4\n- After while: data_index=1 means bytes up to 4 have been \"processed\"\n\nThe consumed_bytes formula = data_index + {0 or 1}\nBut data_index represents \"bytes loaded past init\" which were at positions 4, 5, 6, ...\nThe total bytes consumed should be: 4 (init) + data_index (extra refills) ... wait no.\n\nActually, data_index tracks something different than I thought!\nLet me re-analyze:\n- Init: loads bytes 0..3 into word positions 31-0. data_index=0.\n- First while in consume: reads data[0+4] = data[4]. data_index becomes 1.\n- Second while: reads data[1+4] = data[5]. data_index becomes 2.\n\nSo data_index = number of bytes loaded AFTER init = number of refills.\nThe formula: `data_index + (bit_index >> 3) + (bit_index > 0 ? 1 : 0)`\n\nThis formula seems to represent: bytes consumed past the first 4 + partial byte.\nBut the caller uses it as the TOTAL bytes consumed from the buffer!\n\nTHAT'S THE BUG! The formula is missing the initial 4 bytes!\n\nWait no... if only 1 bit was consumed:\ndata_index=0, bit_index=1. formula = 0 + 0 + 1 = 1.\nThat's correct: 1 byte consumed from the buffer.\n\nIf 8 bits consumed:\ndata_index=1, bit_index=0. formula = 1 + 0 + 0 = 1.\nThat's correct: 1 byte consumed from buffer.\n\nIf 32 bits consumed:\ndata_index=4, bit_index=0. formula = 4 + 0 + 0 = 4.\nCorrect: 4 bytes consumed.\n\nIf 33 bits consumed:\ndata_index=4, bit_index=1. formula = 4 + 0 + 1 = 5.\nCorrect: 5 bytes consumed (ceil(33/8) = 5).\n\nHmm, so the formula IS correct for the NUMBER of bytes consumed from the start. The question is whether data_index can be INFLATED beyond what's warranted.\n\nAnd YES IT CAN! Because data_index increments even when data_index+4 >= size (i.e., no actual read). This means data_index can grow past size-4 and even past size.\n\nBUT: data_index only grows when bit_index >= 8 (the while loop condition). And bit_index grows only through actual consume(n_bits) calls. So data_index = floor(total_consumed_bits / 8).\n\nThe formula: consumed_bytes = floor(total_consumed_bits/8) + (total_consumed_bits%8 > 0 ? 1 : 0) = ceil(total_consumed_bits/8).\n\nThis is ALWAYS the correct number of bytes consumed if total_consumed_bits is correct. So consumed_bytes should equal ceil(total_consumed_bits/8).\n\nFor consumed_bytes > size: need ceil(total/8) > size, i.e., total > size*8.\n\nOK so my original analysis was right. We need total consumed bits > size*8.\nThe fix caps consumed_bits at size*8 and changes the init/refill logic.\n\nBut how can total consumed bits exceed size*8?\nAfter all real bits are consumed, word becomes 0 (unrefilled positions). Zeros don't match codes -> break. So how?\n\nAH WAIT! I think the issue is in the INIT function. In the OLD code:\n```c\nfor (i = 0; i < size && i < 4; i++)\n    word |= (data[i] << ((3 - i) << 3));\n```\n\nThis loads min(size, 4) bytes. With size >= 4: loads 4 bytes (32 bits).\nThe word now has 32 bits of real data.\n\nIn the consume function, the condition for refill is: `data_index + 4 < size`.\nWith size >= 5 and data_index=0: 0+4=4 < size (if size>=5). Reads data[4].\nSo byte 4 is a REFILL byte, adding 8 more bits to word.\n\nBut here's the thing: the TOTAL bits in the word at any time is 32 (it's a 32-bit integer). When we refill, we OR bits into the LOWER positions of word (bit_index position). The upper bits are being consumed. So refill adds fresh data into the bottom of the word as the top is consumed.\n\nAfter all bytes are exhausted (data_index+4 >= size), no more refills. But data_index still increments. And the word retains whatever was already loaded that hasn't been consumed yet.\n\nThe word could still have valid bit patterns from previously loaded bytes! Those patterns could match codes and cause MORE consumption!\n\nHere's the scenario:\n1. Init loads bytes 0-3 (32 bits).\n2. Process some bits (consuming them). After 8 bits: refill loads byte 4 (if size>4).\n3. After byte 4 loaded, no more refills. But word still has bits from bytes 1-4.\n4. Continue processing: consume more bits. data_index keeps going up.\n5. Eventually process all bits from bytes 0-4 (40 bits for size=5).\n6. After all 40 bits: word=0. No more codes match.\n\nTotal consumed = 40. data_index = 5. consumed_bytes = 5 = size. Exact.\n\nBUT WHAT IF: The word doesn't become 0 after consuming all \"real\" bits? What if the refill adds bits in a position that OVERLAPS with bits that are still being processed?\n\nIn the old code's consume:\n```c\nmmr->word <<= n_bits;    // Shift word left by n_bits\nmmr->bit_index += n_bits;\nwhile (mmr->bit_index >= 8) {\n    mmr->bit_index -= 8;\n    if (mmr->data_index + 4 < mmr->size)\n        mmr->word |= (mmr->data[mmr->data_index + 4] << mmr->bit_index);\n    mmr->data_index++;\n}\n```\n\nThe refill ORs data byte into word at position bit_index. After the while iteration:\nbit_index has been decremented by 8. The new bit_index tells where the next refill goes.\n\nFor the first iteration in a consume(24) call:\nbit_index starts at old + 24 (could be large like 24).\nFirst iter: bit_index = 24-8 = 16. Refill goes to position 16.\nSecond iter: bit_index = 16-8 = 8. Refill at position 8.\nThird iter: bit_index = 8-8 = 0. Ref